ion findings that change the story (synthetic BT, all labelled synthetic): (i) log k\\* ≈ 1.1 log z + 0.45 log N (theory: 1 and 0.5); (ii) under exact refit Q = k\\*·g_max/gap is **not** ≥ 1 (≈ 0.97 for deletion, ≈ 1.2 for outcome flips, density-dependent), so F = Q·z is the name kept only for \"SE-unit fragility\", and Q is read only against its own same-graph null; (iii) the Brief's noise curve was wrong: deleting a fraction f of the observed sample gives P_flip(f) = Φ(−z√((1−f)/f)), so the random-noise fraction f_q = z²/(z²+c_q²) is below 0.8 only for z ≲ 2 at q = 16%; (iv) on 53 exhaustive tiny problems iterated re-linearisation = Frank–Wolfe = exact minimum, first-order k_lin/k\\* median 1.26 (deletion), 0.97 (flip).\n76:- **C1 scale.** Per dataset and family, Δ = mean over pairs in the declared z-range of log Q_real − log Q_null against the dataset's own null. Equivalence (TOST-type) rule: \"equivalent to the null\" only if the whole 95% interval lies inside the stated margin, \"differs\" if wholly beyond, else \"inconclusive at this size\". Equivalence is a statement of practical size: if the interval also excludes 0 the paper says \"within the margin, not zero\" and states the size as a ratio exp(Δ); it never writes \"equals the null\" for it. Datasets with few pairs (NBA seasons, MT-Bench) will often be \"inconclusive\" for width alone; the paper reports them as such with their interval widths. \"F collapses on z\" is claimed only for the datasets equivalent to the null (count and share given). Exponents of log k\\* on log z and log N are reported as measured, not as \"∝ z√N\"; raw k\\*/N falls with N by the stated subsampling-slope rule.\n91:5. **Experiments.** 5.0 data and preprocessing (Table 1). 5.1 headline fragility per dataset, the null prediction and the scale (Fig. 1, Table 2). 5.2 CI vs robustness curves (Fig. 3). 5.3 design vs statistics with matched contrasts (Fig. 4). 5.4 predictive rule (Table 3). 5.5 search validity (compact Table 5 in main; full table and Fig. 5 in the appendix). 5.6 sensitivity checks (cost 2 for win→loss, per-turn MT-Bench, home-advantage NBA, tie rate).\n110:| **Table 5** | Search-method comparison on the same conclusions, budget and cost unit (C6). **Compact version in the main text** (4–5 rows: random at equal budget, one-shot influence top-k, iterated linear + refit, exact / lower bound where available; columns: median k\\* as a ratio to the best found, share flipped at 0.5k\\* and at k\\*); the full table in the appendix | per-method smallest verified k\\*, flipped share at b |\n114:**Main body vs appendix, decided now** so the page limit does not shape results later. Main: Table 1 (compact), Table 2, Figs. 1, 3, 4 (the former Fig. 2 is a panel of Fig. 1; the numbering of Figs. 3–4 is kept to avoid confusing the neighbors), the **compact Table 5** (the reviewers' first question is whether one-shot influence already gives the number, and the paper's justification for its own search is the answer), and a short paragraph in 4.4/5.5 giving the numbers the search-validity claim needs (not a pointer to the appendix: the venue says the paper must stand alone). The synthetic calibration gets a paragraph in the method because it fixes the margins and the noise formula. Appendix: Table 3 (predictive rule: moves to main as a sentence plus numbers if C5 is claimed), Table 4, full Table 5, Fig.\n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "067708ae-f865-5c4a-af94-ee642e788113", "at": 1795668937.6926103, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 25691, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.session/request_permission", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "5c7e292da3e549b272c9baae7fb6e30e", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "session/request_permission"}, {"id": "7be4a348-3c90-5603-8505-334598c8fa3f", "at": 1795668937.7008212, "clock":